<title>ESTONIA: GOVERNMENT OK'S TAX BREAKS FOR INVESTORS - REPORT.</title>
<headline>GOVERNMENT OK'S TAX BREAKS FOR INVESTORS - REPORT.</headline>
<dateline>TALLINN 1997-04-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>The government has decided in principle to allow companies a tax break of one quarter of their profits if it is reinvested, Aripaev business newspaper said on Friday.</p>
<p>The decision, which still requires legislation to be drawn up and parliamentary approval, would allow companies that invest one quarter of their profits into fixed assets to deduct the invested amount from their tax bill.</p>
<p>A finance ministry official said the new regulations would take effect in 1998 and apply to 1997's profits, the newspaper added.</p>
<p>The ministry hopes to introduce the required bill to parliament before the summer break, the newspaper added.</p>
<p>-- David Mardiste, Tallinn newsroom +372 630 8400 reuters@reuters.ee</p>
</text>
